            </div>
          </div>

          <p>
            The four flavors are close to identical on the panel. Per the brand's nutrition facts images at the time of writing, every flavor gives 750 to 770 calories, 52 to 55 g of protein and 107 to 111 g of carbohydrate, with 15 servings per tub. Chocolate Peanut Butter is the only one with added sugar (2 g, from the peanut flour blend) and it has the most protein at 55 g. Strawberry Cheesecake is slightly leaner on fat and uses beet root powder for color.
          </p>

          <p>
            Three details on the label are easy to miss. First, the 12 g of total sugars come mostly from the milk in the whey concentrate and from the maltodextrin breakdown, not from table sugar, and the panel shows 0 g added sugar on three of the four flavors. Second, the 12 g of saturated fat per serving comes from the coconut milk powder and MCT oil, which is 60% of the Daily Value. Third, sodium caseinate appears inside the MCT powder, so the product contains a small amount of casein as well as whey.
          </p>

          <p>
            The protein is a single source: whey concentrate from grass-fed cows, per Transparent Labs. That is different from <a href="/serious-mass-protein-powder-review/">Serious Mass</a> and Naked Mass, which blend in casein for slower digestion. Whey concentrate still contains some lactose, so the product is not lactose-free, and the label declares milk plus coconut (listed as tree nuts on the Chocolate Glaze panel), with peanuts in the Chocolate Peanut Butter flavor.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="third-party-tested" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            4. Is Transparent Labs Mass Gainer Third-Party Tested?
          </h2>

          <p>
            Yes, according to Transparent Labs: the brand says every batch of Mass Gainer is tested by a third-party lab for purity and potency, and that the product is certified by Informed Choice. The front of the tub, as shown on the brand's product images at the time of writing, prints both an Informed Choice seal and an Informed Protein seal. Transparent Labs also says it publishes its lab results through a link in its website footer.
          </p>

          <p>
            Those are two different checks. Informed Choice is a program run by LGC that tests supplements for substances banned in sport, so it matters most to drug-tested athletes. Informed Protein checks that the protein in the tub matches the protein on the label, which is a direct answer to the amino-spiking problem that hit some protein brands years ago. Neither program is a heavy-metal certification, so neither tells you how much lead or cadmium is in a serving.
          </p>

          <p>
            We tried to confirm the listing in the Informed Choice public database for this review, but the registry blocked automated access when we checked on 20 November 2026, so we are reporting the certification as a brand claim backed by the seals printed on the label. If you compete in a tested sport, search the product name and lot number on the Informed Choice site before you buy. Certification is granted product by product, and the listing is what an anti-doping body will look at.
          </p>

          <p>
            For comparison, the main rivals are tested less formally. Naked says Naked Mass is NSF Contents Certified, which checks contents but is not NSF Certified for Sport. Optimum Nutrition's US Serious Mass label shows no Informed Choice or NSF Sport mark. Dymatize does not state third-party certification on the Super Mass Amazon listing we checked. That makes Transparent Labs the best-documented gainer of the four, which is the main reason to pay its price. Our <a href="/transparent-labs-protein-powder-review/">Transparent Labs protein review</a> covers how the rest of the brand's range is certified.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-3">THE RULE</p>
            <p class="text-xl font-bold text-white mb-3">A seal on the tub is a claim; a database listing is proof.</p>
            <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed m-0">Transparent Labs prints Informed Choice and Informed Protein seals on Mass Gainer. Tested athletes should still look the product up on the Informed Choice site, because certification applies to specific products and batches.</p>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div id="calories" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            5. Why Does Transparent Labs Mass Gainer Have 770 Calories Instead of 1,250?
          </h2>

          <p>
            Transparent Labs Mass Gainer has about 770 calories per serving because it is designed around protein and fat rather than as many carbohydrates as possible. Big-bag gainers such as Serious Mass and Dymatize Super Mass reach 1,250 to 1,290 calories by packing 245 to 253 g of carbohydrate into each serving. Transparent Labs stops at 107 to 111 g and adds more protein and some fat instead.
          </p>

          <p>
            The protein share shows the difference. In Transparent Labs Mass Gainer, the 53 g of protein supplies about 212 of the 770 calories, or roughly 28% of the shake. In Serious Mass, 50 g of protein supplies about 200 of 1,260 calories, or about 16%. Put another way, Transparent Labs has roughly two grams of carbohydrate for every gram of protein, while Serious Mass and Super Mass sit closer to five to one.
          </p>

          <p>
            That design has real advantages. A 199 g serving is easier to drink than a 321 g or 340 g one, and many buyers find smaller gainers sit better in the stomach. The protein dose is high enough to count as a proper post-workout serving on its own. And with fewer carbohydrates per shake, it is easier to add the gainer to normal meals without overshooting your calorie target on rest days.
          </p>

          <p>
            The cost is that hard gainers may not get enough. If you have tracked your food and need 1,000 or more extra calories a day to gain, one serving of Transparent Labs will leave a gap. You can blend it with 16 oz of whole milk, which per the USDA adds roughly 300 calories and 16 g of protein, or add oats, a banana or peanut butter. Doubling up is an option but doubles the creatine and saturated fat too.
          </p>

          <p>
            For many people, though, 770 calories is closer to the right number than 1,250. A typical lean bulk aims for a surplus of a few hundred calories a day, and a gainer that delivers the whole surplus in one shake makes it easy to gain fat faster than muscle. Our explainer on <a href="/mass-gainer-vs-protein-powder/">mass gainer vs protein powder</a> walks through how to work out which one your diet actually needs.
          </p>
        </div>

