        <div id="healthy" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            9. Is Transparent Labs Mass Gainer Healthy? Calories, Blood Sugar and Kidney Caveats
          </h2>

          <p>
            Transparent Labs Mass Gainer is a reasonable choice for healthy adults who need extra calories, but it is a concentrated source of calories, fast carbohydrate and saturated fat, so it is not a health food for everyone. It has no artificial sweeteners or dyes, per the brand, and 0 g of added sugar on three flavors. That makes it cleaner than most gainers. Clean is not the same as right for you, and a few groups should check with a doctor first.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Calories</h3>

          <p>
            A serving gives 750 to 770 calories, which is more than many people's lunch. If you are not tracking intake and training hard, those calories can easily turn into fat gain rather than muscle. Gainers make sense when you have measured your food and know you are short. If you mostly need protein, a regular protein powder such as the brand's grass-fed whey isolate is a better fit.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Blood sugar</h3>

          <p>
            About 100 g of each serving is organic tapioca maltodextrin, a fast-digesting starch with a high glycemic index. Even with 0 g of added sugar on the panel, that will raise blood glucose quickly. People with type 1 or type 2 diabetes, prediabetes or insulin resistance should talk to their doctor or a registered dietitian before using any mass gainer, and should not assume that a stevia-sweetened gainer is low-carb. It is not.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Kidney disease, saturated fat and creatine</h3>

          <p>
            Each serving has 53 g of protein and 3 g of creatine. People with chronic kidney disease are often told to limit protein, and creatine raises blood creatinine, a marker doctors use to judge kidney function, so anyone with kidney disease should not use this product without medical advice. The 12 g of saturated fat is 60% of the Daily Value; the US Dietary Guidelines suggest keeping saturated fat under 10% of daily calories, so people with high cholesterol or heart disease should factor it in.
          </p>

          <p>
            Pregnant or breastfeeding people, teenagers and anyone taking medication should check with a doctor or registered dietitian too, mainly because of the creatine and the calorie load. Our guide to <a href="/is-protein-powder-safe/">whether protein powder is safe</a> explains what the different testing programs do and do not cover.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="who-should-buy" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            10. Who Should Buy Transparent Labs Mass Gainer, and Who Should Skip It?
          </h2>

          <p>
            Transparent Labs Mass Gainer suits lifters on a lean bulk who want a certified, stevia-sweetened gainer with creatine, and who need roughly 500 to 800 extra calories a day. It also suits people who find 1,250-calorie gainers too heavy to drink or too hard on their stomach. If that sounds like you, it is one of the best-built gainers available.
          </p>

          <p>
            Skip it if you need more than about 1,000 extra calories a day and are on a budget; Dymatize Super Mass or plain food will get you there for less. Skip it if you already take a separate creatine supplement and do not want the extra dose, or if you would rather avoid stevia altogether, in which case unflavored Naked Mass is the better fit. And skip it if your real shortfall is protein, not calories.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">How to use it</h3>

          <p>
            Transparent Labs recommends two heaping scoops in 16 oz of water or milk, ideally after training or as a meal replacement on rest days. It suggests starting with half a serving, one scoop, for the first week to see how your digestion handles it, then moving to the full serving. Many buyers stay on one scoop, which gives roughly 385 calories and 26 to 27 g of protein and makes a tub last 30 days.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Our verdict</h3>

          <p>
            This is the gainer we would pick for someone who cares what goes in their shake and has a moderate calorie target. It costs more per calorie than the big-bag gainers, but you get a better protein share, a printed dose for every ingredient, creatine and an Informed Choice claim. For a ranked look at every option, see our <a href="/best-protein-powder-for-weight-gain/">best protein powder for weight gain</a> roundup.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- Common Mistakes -->
        <div id="mistakes" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            11. Common Mistakes to Avoid
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Assuming 0 g added sugar means low-carb</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                The panel shows 0 g of added sugar on three flavors, but each serving still carries 107 to 111 g of carbohydrate, about 100 g of it tapioca maltodextrin. That is a fast-digesting starch with a high glycemic index. Anyone watching blood sugar should count it as a large carbohydrate load, not a sugar-free shake.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Doubling the serving to match 1,250-calorie gainers</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Two full servings give about 1,540 calories, but also 6 g of creatine, 24 g of saturated fat and over 1,000 mg of sodium. If you need that many calories, add whole milk, oats or peanut butter to one serving instead, or buy a higher-calorie gainer built for that job.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Stacking it with a separate creatine scoop</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Each full serving already contains 3 g of creatine monohydrate, which sits in the commonly studied 3 to 5 g daily range. Adding your usual 5 g scoop on top rarely helps. If you take half servings, a small top-up is reasonable; otherwise drop the separate creatine while you use the gainer.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Treating the seal as proof for every batch</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Transparent Labs prints Informed Choice and Informed Protein seals and publishes lab results, but tested athletes should still look up the product on the Informed Choice site. Certification is product specific, and the registry listing, not the tub, is what counts if a test is ever questioned.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
